  "prompt_text": "This figure is a simple conceptual diagram showing the main physical ingredients that govern an interaction between two species, labeled A and B, in the center. The central box suggests a pairwise interaction or transfer between A and B, with one row showing the two partners connected and another showing a directional arrow from B toward A, implying coupling or charge/electron movement between them.\n\nAround this central interaction are four boxed factors that feed into it. “Electrostatics” is represented with plus and minus symbols, indicating attraction or repulsion due to charge. “Pauli” is shown with opposite-spin arrows, pointing to Pauli exclusion and short-range repulsion arising from overlapping electrons. “Orbitals” is illustrated with overlapping lobes, emphasizing the role of orbital shape and overlap in bonding or interaction strength. “Dispersion” is depicted with fluctuating partial charges, indicating weak induced-dipole attractions.\n\nTaken together, the figure conveys that the interaction between A and B is not controlled by a single effect, but by the balance of several standard components of intermolecular or interatomic interactions: electrostatic forces, orbital interactions, Pauli repulsion, and dispersion.",
